Expected Outcomes
A running Camel-on-Spring-Boot application where public, user, and admin endpoints enforce distinct access levels via HTTP Basic Authentication with real password verification
Confirmed rejection (HTTP 401/403) of missing, incorrect, or insufficiently privileged credentials
A working AES encryption/decryption round trip performed inside Camel routes, with sensitive data never stored or logged in plaintext
Troubleshooting
Error: Invalid AES key length or java.security.InvalidKeyException: Invalid key length when calling /api/user/encrypt Fix: Confirm app.security.encryption.key in application.yml is exactly 16 characters (for AES-128). Count the characters and adjust, then restart with mvn clean spring-boot:run.

Error: curl: (7) Failed to connect to localhost port 8080: Connection refused Fix: The application has not finished starting or is not running. Re-run mvn spring-boot:run in a dedicated terminal and wait for the Tomcat started on port(s): 8080 log line before testing in another terminal.

Error: HTTP 401 Unauthorized returned even with correct-looking credentials Fix: Verify the -u flag uses the exact case-sensitive username and password (user:user123 or admin:admin123) with no extra spaces, and that the request targets http://localhost:8080 not https://.

Conclusion
In this lab, students built a secure Apache Camel application on Spring Boot that enforces HTTP Basic Authentication with genuine, Spring Security-verified password checks, applies role-based authorization across public, user, and admin endpoints, and performs AES-based encryption and decryption of sensitive data directly within Camel routes. By testing each endpoint with valid, invalid, and insufficiently privileged credentials, students confirmed that authentication and authorization controls actually reject unauthorized access rather than merely appearing to. These practices, credential verification, least-privilege route access, and encryption of sensitive payloads, form a foundation for securing integration flows in real enterprise Camel deployments.
